<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lands of Overmaas</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lands of Overmaas were territories east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maastricht">Maastricht</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a>: the lordships of Valkenburg, Dalhem and 's-Hertogenrade (Herzogenrath), often counted together with the neighbouring Duchy of Limburg. They were ruled by the dukes of Brabant from the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and later by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>. Disputed in the Eighty Years' War, they were divided between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> by the Partition Treaty of 1661.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lands-of-overmaas.jpg" alt="Lands of Overmaas" width="320" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chipmunkdavis · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Locator_Lands_of_Overmaas_(1350).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The name, Landen van Overmaas in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and Pays d'Outre-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> in French, means the countries over the Meuse: seen from the residence of the dukes of Brabant in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>, the lordships lay on the far, right bank of the river. Besides the Lands of Valkenburg, 's-Hertogenrade and Dalhem they included the towns of Gangelt and Waldfeucht, between the cities of Liège, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maastricht">Maastricht</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a>.</p><p>Until the end of the 18th century three languages were in use. The people spoke a language of the German-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> dialect continuum that cannot be labelled as either German or Dutch, while Dutch was the language of government and High German that of schools and churches. The area remained Catholic after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> and never received a large influx of Calvinists, as nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maastricht">Maastricht</a> did.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Until the end of the 13th century the lordships belonged to the Duchy of Limburg. The death of its last independent ruler, Duchess Ermingarde, in 1283 led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-limburg-succession">War of the Limburg Succession</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-duke-of-brabant">John I, Duke of Brabant</a> won at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-worringen">Battle of Worringen</a> in 1288; the name Lands of Overmaas dates from this acquisition. The dukes often pledged the Lands to creditors, and late in the 14th century they were held by John of Gronsfeld and Rainald of Schoonvorst until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-bold">Philip the Bold</a>, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> bought out the debts in 1388 and 1389.</p><p>This opened the way for the Valois dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> to acquire Brabant itself. Through marriage the Burgundian Netherlands passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-habsburg">House of Habsburg</a> in 1482, and later to its Spanish branch. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> conquered the area in 1632, during the Eighty Years' War, and its partition created what the historian Benjamin J. Kaplan called a complex patchwork, some districts forming isolated islands. The Dutch part was governed among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/generality-lands">Generality Lands</a>, where, unlike anywhere else in the Republic, Calvinists and Catholics had to share churches.</p><p>Revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> annexed the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a> in 1795 and abolished feudal rights, ending the Lands as lordships; they were divided between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ourthe">Ourthe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse-inferieure">Meuse-Inférieure</a>. After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> in 1815 the area was partitioned between the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Kingdom of the Netherlands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine-province">Rhenish Prussia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lands of Overmaas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1661 the <b>Overmaas</b> lands were divided with the Dutch Republic.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-limburg">Duchy of Limburg</a></li></ul></section>
